---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const lima = assertion('lima-reads-at-forty-two-per-cent')
const fell = assertion('twelve-districts-fell-in-the-same-two-years')
const passed = assertion('the-county-passed-the-state-without-improving')
const legible = assertion('the-only-district-published-as-a-number')
const bounds = assertion('the-county-graduation-rate-fell-and-the-states-rose')
const race = assertion('no-racial-gap-in-limas-graduation-rate')
const series = assertion('limas-graduation-rate-has-not-moved')

const spec = (a: typeof lima, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const limaSpec = spec(lima, 'Per cent proficient in reading, 2018')
const fellSpec = spec(fell, 'Points lost in reading, 2013 to 2015')
const passedSpec = spec(passed, 'Points above Ohio')
const legibleSpec = spec(legible, 'District-years of graduation data')
const boundsSpec = spec(bounds, 'Graduation rate, per cent')
const seriesSpec = spec(series, 'Lima City graduation rate, per cent')
---

<Article slug="the-only-legible-district">
  <p>
    Allen County's twelve school districts have been in this corpus for a while: how many children
    are in them, how many teachers, how poor the children are, what each district spends and where
    the money comes from. What was missing was the thing a parent asks first. Districts report their
    results to the state, states report to the federal government, and the federal government
    publishes by district — ten years of graduation rates and ten of test scores.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lima} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="lima"
    title="Reading proficiency, 2018"
    caption="Share of tested pupils scoring proficient, all grades together. Every figure rests on between 250 and 1,904 tests, so none of the ordering is a small-numbers artefact."
    columns={['District', 'Proficient']}
    rows={lima.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(0)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="lima" set:html={JSON.stringify(limaSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Lima City is the county's largest district, its poorest, and its highest spender per pupil. It is
    also twenty points below the second-lowest district and forty-one below the highest, in both
    subjects. The corpus records that spending, poverty and results move together here and declines
    to put them in an order.
  </p>

  <h2>A fall that arrived everywhere at once</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={fell} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="fell"
    title="Reading proficiency lost between 2013 and 2015"
    caption="Four of the twelve. Every district in the county fell, by between 18 and 40 points, and the Ohio average fell 28.7 over the same two years."
    columns={['District', 'Points lost']}
    rows={fell.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(0)}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="fell" set:html={JSON.stringify(fellSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Twelve independently governed districts, with different boards, different budgets and different
    children, do not lose a fifth to two fifths of their proficiency in the same two years unless
    something outside all of them changed. That something is the test. Which test replaced which,
    and on whose authority, is not in this collection, and the corpus has opened it rather than
    guessed.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={passed} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="passed"
    title="Allen County against Ohio"
    caption="Points of proficiency above the state average, weighting each district by the tests it gave. In 2009 the county was 0.8 points below Ohio in reading; it is now above."
    columns={['Subject', 'Above Ohio']}
    rows={passed.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="passed" set:html={JSON.stringify(passedSpec)} is:inline />

  <h2>What the file will not show you</h2>
  <p>
    Graduation rates are a different matter, and the difference is arithmetic. A district gives about
    nine thousand tests a year and graduates about thirteen hundred pupils, and a federal disclosure
    rule that leaves the first alone bites hard on the second.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={legible} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="legible"
    title="How a graduation rate is published"
    caption="The county's twelve districts over ten years. A banded rate reads 90-94, or 95-100, or 50-100; an exact rate reads 63."
    columns={['Form', 'District-years']}
    rows={legible.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(0)}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="legible" set:html={JSON.stringify(legibleSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Across Ohio, no district with a graduating cohort under 150 has ever had an exact rate published,
    and the smallest exact cohort in ten years is 201. So the districts a reader can read are the
    large ones — and in this county the large district is the city district, the poor one, and the
    one with the worst rate. The single number among eleven bands is 63 per cent, and it belongs to
    Lima.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={series} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="series"
    title="Lima City's graduation rate"
    caption="Six of the ten years. It is the only rate in Allen County published as a number in every year of the run, and the only one that can be tracked at all."
    columns={['Year', 'Rate']}
    rows={series.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(0)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="series" set:html={JSON.stringify(seriesSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Tracked across the decade it barely moves: nine of the ten years fall between 63 and 70 per cent,
    and the exception is 2011. Everywhere else in the county a band four or five points wide would
    hide a change of that size completely.
  </p>
  <Gloss>
    That is worth naming as a rule, because it is not the rule for a survey margin. A sampling margin
    is symmetric and probabilistic; a disclosure band contains the truth with certainty and has no
    shape inside it, and its width is set by the size of the unit rather than the size of a sample.
    So: print the band, never the midpoint column — 173 of this county's graduation rows read 50 to
    100 and every one carries a midpoint of 75 — weight bounds into an interval, and assert a
    difference only where two intervals fail to touch.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={bounds} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="bounds"
    title="The county's graduation rate, bounded"
    caption="Each district's published band weighted by its own cohort. The 2011 and 2019 intervals do not overlap, so the fall is established without any assumption about where inside a band a district sits."
    columns={['Bound', 'Per cent']}
    rows={bounds.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="bounds" set:html={JSON.stringify(boundsSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Against 2010 rather than 2011 the same two intervals overlap by seven tenths of a point, and
    nothing is established at all. The county's advantage over the state has gone the same way: clear
    of Ohio in every year to 2015, overlapping it since 2016, with the state's band the higher of the
    two in 2019.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={race} />
  <p>
    One last thing the bands permit. Lima's cohorts are large enough to be published by race, and
    across ten years the white and Black graduation bands are disjoint seven times, with white higher
    in three and Black higher in four. On housing cost and on mortgage lending this county shows a
    gap that survives every test put to it. On finishing high school in its largest district, there
    is no gap to find.
  </p>
</Article>
